---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Gallery from '../../components/Gallery.astro';
---

<Layout title="'Suburbia' — PVP Shooter Level — Christopher St George">
  <article class="project-page">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">&larr; Back to all projects</a>

    <h1>PVP Shooter Level</h1>
    <p class="project-meta">
      'Suburbia' &middot; Level Designer (Solo)
    </p>

    <div class="project-body">
      <div class="project-text">
        <section>
          <h2>Overview</h2>
          <p>
            Suburbia is a 5v5 Valorant plant/defuse map that was developed over
            the course of 4 weeks as an individual assessment piece for QUT's
            Immersive Game Level Design subject.
          </p>
          <p>
            For this project, I wanted to challenge myself to work within the
            predefined limits of an asset pack without supplementing it with
            bespoke assets. All assets in the project were sourced from the
            <a href="https://www.fab.com/listings/fd558d8c-bd7e-461f-8449-a7cc9c277078" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Assetsville Town</a>
            asset pack.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>What I did</h2>
          <p>
            As a lifelong Counterstrike player, I of course had to start by
            downloading Valorant and playing a few games to familiarise myself
            with the maps and the key differences. The biggest change from CS,
            are the agent abilities — these are far more impactful than any of
            the utility that you have access to in CS. After getting to know
            the game (and absolutely crushing it in Unranked mode), I looked
            through the top-down diagrams of all existing Valorant maps on the
            <a href="https://playvalorant.com/en-us/maps/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Valorant website</a>.
            I knew I couldn't match the iterative design capability of a full
            AAA team, so I picked three maps that met my two criteria: 3-lanes,
            and a long mid lane. These ended up being Ascent, Corrode, and
            Summit.
          </p>
          <p>Next, I identified the key points that all these maps had in common:</p>
          <ol>
            <li>Two entrances from defender-spawn&gt;site.</li>
            <li>One entrance from attacker-spawn&gt;site. One entrance from attacker spawn&gt;mid&gt;site.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            And incorporated these into my level. I particularly wanted to play
            around with a longer mid-section, as I felt that mid had been far
            less impactful in my Valorant matches than it was in CS, and as
            such, weapons like the Operator sniper hadn't gotten a lot of use.
          </p>
          <p>
            The level gimmick I had in mind was a central room in between the
            two plant sites that had operable doors, so players could open or
            shut them (with a cooldown). The narrative I concocted to
            accommodate this gimmick was that the suburb contained a secret
            Radianite-powered factory hidden within a warehouse complex that the
            attacking team was attempting to seize in order to siphon the
            Radianite.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Tools &amp; technologies</h2>
          <ul class="tag-list">
            <li>Unreal</li>
            <li>Balsamiq</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </div>

      <div class="project-gallery">
        <Gallery
          images={[
            { src: '/images/project-3-map-overview.png', alt: 'Suburbia top-down layout, all three design drafts side by side' },
            { src: '/images/project-3-map-draft-1.png', alt: 'Suburbia Draft 1 top-down layout' },
            { src: '/images/project-3-map-draft-2.png', alt: 'Suburbia Draft 2 top-down layout' },
            { src: '/images/project-3-map-draft-3.png', alt: 'Suburbia Draft 3 top-down layout' },
          ]}
        />

